  "worked_solution": "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{{2}^{n-1}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=1,$ $r=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $N=6.$ If we substitute these values into the above formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{{2}^{n-1}} & =1\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{63}{64})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
